{"kind":"promise","text":"Promised. Empty inherited set, the real trust scope named in code and on the page, the platform contract narrowed per your ruling, --no-includes on the local read, every existing regression and control kept passing and mutation-sensitive. Then review from codex. I will not merge.\n\nYOUR PLATFORM RULING IS THE RULE ONE LEVEL UP AND I WANT TO SAY SO. I had been applying \"state only what you exercised\" to behaviour and not to the platform the behaviour was exercised on. A closed-environment claim is a claim about an execution environment, and naming the property without naming where it was demonstrated is the same overstatement in a place I had not thought to look. So the page will say the boundary is closed on Unix, where it is exercised, and will name Go's SYSTEMROOT inheritance on Windows as outside that contract rather than as a caveat inside it.\n\nWHAT I WILL NOT DO, since you named it. Write a Windows boundary regression I cannot run. A test that has never executed is not evidence, and shipping one alongside a claim would give the claim a false witness — which is worse than the bare claim, because the next reader would stop checking.\n\nON THE EMPTY SET. Removing PATH should make the case-folding comparison and its duplicate-name edge dead, and I expect to delete them. If something still needs that machinery I will say what rather than leave it in place looking load-bearing; an allowlist with nothing in it should not keep the apparatus of admission.\n\nON --no-includes. This is the one part that narrows behaviour rather than restating it, so it gets checked rather than assumed: the local read exists to find remote.origin.url, and if any real repository configures its remotes through an include then adding the flag would break reading them. I will find out rather than reason about it, and if it does break that case I will report it instead of shipping the flag.\n\nON THE FIFTH INSTANCE. You are right that I read the implementer's comment naming the SYSTEMROOT behaviour and wrote the absolute sentence in the artifact anyway. The gap is not that I lacked the fact — it is that I write artifact prose from what I intended the change to achieve rather than from what the code in front of me does, and the two diverge exactly where I have not personally exercised something. The correction that would actually work is to draft artifact claims from the code and the measurements, and treat my intent as a hypothesis the evidence has to support, rather than the other way round."}